1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:27,800
HOST: When Columbus reached the island of Hispaniola, more than a million Taíno people farmed, fished, and raised families there. Within decades, by one Spanish count, only a few hundred were left.  Conquest is a story about soldiers. But empire is a story about workers. Silver had to be mined. Sugar had to be cut. And the Spanish Empire ran on one question: who would do the work?

2
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:46,799
HOST: By the end: One — how encomienda granted and justified claims to Native labor. Two — how disease and overwork produced a labor shortage. Three — economic logic from a dying Native workforce to the slave trade. Four — casta ordering colonial society by ancestry.

3
00:00:46,799 --> 00:00:54,799
HOST: Spain's first answer was the encomienda[/es]. Here is how it was supposed to work.

4
00:00:54,799 --> 00:01:06,599
GRANT: To our loyal settler, we grant the labor of the Indians of this district. In return, he shall protect them, and instruct them in our holy faith.

5
00:01:06,599 --> 00:01:42,399
HOST: Protection and Christianity. That was the deal.  The practice was different. The encomenderos[/es] ruled like feudal lords. They worked whole villages to death in the mines and the fields, and sent the Crown its cut of the silver.  A friar named Bartolomé de Las Casas saw it and wrote it down. His book, written in 1542, shocked Spain. The Crown answered with the repartimiento[/es] reforms of 1542. No more private grants. The Crown would assign the labor directly.  The treatment did not improve. But the control did.

6
00:01:42,399 --> 00:02:04,599
HOST: And then the workers started dying. Smallpox, influenza, measles. Diseases no one on these continents had ever met. Between 1500 and 1650, somewhere between half and nine in ten Native people in the Americas died. On Hispaniola, overwork finished what disease started.  Las Casas walked through the ruins and wrote what he saw.

7
00:02:04,599 --> 00:02:18,000
LASCASAS: I saw them perish in the mines, in the fields, on the roads. Husbands died, wives died, children died. The land emptied, and there was no one left to work it.

8
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:26,000
HOST: An empire built on forced labor, with no one left to force. Spain needed a new answer.

9
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:34,000
HOST: The new answer crossed the Atlantic in chains.

10
00:02:34,000 --> 00:03:22,199
HOST: Spain turned to enslaved African labor, bought through the trade the Portuguese had already built to feed Europe's sugar islands. They carried greater resistance to Old World diseases, and the Spanish saw them as replaceable through the trade, torn from their homelands and easier to control.  And here the story takes a darker turn. Slavery was ancient. But starting in the 1500s, it changed. Now it was for life. Now the children of slaves were slaves. Now a human being was property, with no rights at all.  That idea, that a person could be owned the way you own a tool, became the engine of the new labor system. And it would outlive the Spanish Empire by centuries.

11
00:03:22,199 --> 00:04:10,799
HOST: On top of the labor system, Spain built a social order to match.  Look at this painting. A Mexican artist, in 1763, painted the colony the way it saw itself. What is it telling you? [2s pause] It is a ladder. At the top, peninsulares[/es], born in Spain, and criollos[/es], Spaniards born in the Americas. One or two in a hundred people. Below them, mestizos[/es], then mulatos[/es]. At the bottom, Indians and Africans.  The Spanish called it the sistema de castas[/es]. It was rooted in an old Spanish obsession, limpieza de sangre[/es], purity of blood. Your ancestors decided your place. And your place decided your work.  In theory rigid. In practice, people moved, mixed, and bought their way up.

12
00:04:10,799 --> 00:04:20,799
